# secrets/.gitattributes — git-crypt encryption boundary for this folder
# (SPEC.md §8.1 "Secrets (decided design)": "`/flake/secrets/` is a
# git-crypt-encrypted folder ... encrypted in the repo and on any remote,
# plaintext in the working tree for keyholders"; GitHub issue #79,
# milestone M4 groundwork — installer flow wiring that MATERIALIZES a real
# `secrets/` directory like this one into a machine's own `/flake` is M7,
# out of scope here. This file + `index.nix` are the TEMPLATE a future
# installer copies verbatim (SPEC.md §4.2: "The machine's configuration
# flake lives at `/flake` ... with git-crypt protecting the secrets
# file"); the mechanism they encode is real and exercised today by
# tests/unit/166-secrets-gitcrypt-gitattributes.sh and
# tests/unit/168-secrets-gitcrypt-roundtrip.sh.
#
# -- The default: everything under secrets/ is git-crypt-encrypted --------
#
# git-crypt's own convention (see `git-crypt --help` / its README): a path
# is encrypted at rest IFF its `.gitattributes` `filter`/`diff` attributes
# are set to `git-crypt` (via the `git-crypt` clean/smudge filter git-crypt
# itself registers into this repo's git config on `git-crypt init`/`git-crypt
# unlock`). Nothing is encrypted unless a pattern below says so; the
# leading `*` opts every path under this directory in by default, then
# specific exceptions below opt individual paths back OUT.
* filter=git-crypt diff=git-crypt

# -- The two exceptions: this file, and index.nix --------------------------
#
# `.gitattributes` itself must NEVER be encrypted: git needs to read it
# (via the filter/diff attributes it itself declares) before a clone/fetch
# can decide how to treat any other path here at all — an encrypted
# `.gitattributes` would be a chicken-and-egg deadlock for every clone that
# does not already hold a git-crypt key. This is git-crypt's own documented
# convention, not an ubuntnix-specific choice.
.gitattributes !filter !diff

# `index.nix` (SPEC.md §8.1's own `secrets/index.nix`) is left CLEAR
# (plaintext in the repo and on any remote), NOT encrypted — this is this
# issue's own decision, made deliberately, for three independent reasons
# (see docs/secrets.md's "Why index.nix is left clear" section for the
# full writeup, kept here only as a summary the code and the docs both
# point back to):
#
#   1. index.nix carries REFERENCES ONLY (name, owner, group, mode, dst,
#      environmentVariable, and a `src` path — see nix/secrets.nix's own
#      header, "THE ABSOLUTE INVARIANT") — never secret material itself.
#      There is nothing in it that needs at-rest confidentiality; the
#      thing git-crypt exists to protect is exactly the *.key/*-token
#      material files sitting alongside it, which the `*` rule above still
#      covers.
#   2. nix/secrets.nix's `mkManifest` (and, downstream, `bin/ubx-secrets
#      plan`/`ubx rebuild switch|test`) needs to EVALUATE this file's
#      declared shape to render the secrets manifest. Evaluation happens
#      wherever `nix flake check`/`ubx rebuild` runs — which is not
#      guaranteed to be a git-crypt-unlocked keyholder's own machine (CI,
#      a fresh clone before `git-crypt unlock`, `nix flake check` in a
#      sandboxed evaluator with no GPG agent reachable at all). An
#      encrypted index.nix would make the flake fail to evaluate anywhere
#      it is not already unlocked — the one file every consumer of this
#      folder needs to read regardless of keyholder status.
#   3. Diffability and mergeability: git-crypt's own README calls out that
#      encrypted blobs diff/merge as opaque binary noise. index.nix is the
#      one file in this folder that legitimately changes on every ordinary
#      "add a secret"/"rename a secret"/"change an owner" PR — keeping it
#      clear keeps those reviewable as normal text diffs, while the actual
#      material files (added/rotated far less often, and never meaningfully
#      "diffed" even when clear) stay encrypted.
#
# Consequence: index.nix, by design, must never itself carry a raw secret
# value in any field — exactly nix/secrets.nix's own "THE ABSOLUTE
# INVARIANT" already enforces structurally (its rendered JSON manifest has
# a closed field list that never includes `src`'s own bytes, only `src`'s
# path is written to this file at declaration time, and the path takes the
# form `./some-filename`, revealing at most a secret's own chosen filename
# — never its content).
index.nix !filter !diff
